.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt LIBDEVICECMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm devicecmp_open ,
.Nm devicecmp_list
.Nd public client for the system device broker
.Sh LIBRARY
.Li libdevicecmp
.Pq Fl ldevicecmp
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In devicecmp.h
.Ft int
.Fn devicecmp_open "struct service_context *ctx" "const char *name" "uint32_t want_rights" "uint32_t *granted_rights" "int *fdp"
.Ft int
.Fn devicecmp_list "struct service_context *ctx" "uint32_t cursor" "struct devicecmp_list_entry *entries" "uint32_t max" "uint32_t *countp" "uint32_t *next_cursor"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library is the client for the
.Dv system.Device
broker.
The broker opens a named
.Pa /dev
leaf under the caller's switchboard-authenticated, per-label policy and delivers a
rights-limited, non-forwardable descriptor, so a sandboxed component can reach a
device node it cannot open by path itself.
.Pp
.Fn devicecmp_open
opens the
.Pa /dev
leaf named
.Fa name ,
which must be a single
.Pa /dev
component (no
.Ql / ,
no leading
.Ql \&. ,
no
.Ql \&.. ) .
.Fa want_rights
is a mask of
.Dv DEVICECMP_RIGHT_READ , DEVICECMP_RIGHT_WRITE ,
.Dv DEVICECMP_RIGHT_IOCTL , DEVICECMP_RIGHT_MMAP ,
.Dv DEVICECMP_RIGHT_SEEK ,
and
.Dv DEVICECMP_RIGHT_EVENT .
On success the delivered descriptor is stored in
.Fa fdp
(the caller closes it) and, when
.Fa granted_rights
is non-NULL, the rights the provider actually granted \(em
.Fa want_rights
intersected with the per-label policy maximum \(em are stored there.
On failure the call returns \-1 with
.Va errno
set and leaves
.Fa fdp
set to \-1.
The request fails closed.
.Pp
.Fa ctx
is the process
.Xr libservice 3
context and may be
.Dv NULL
for a standalone caller; the provider session is opened by name once and cached
for the process
.Pq a forked child opens its own .
The library speaks the FIDL protocol
.Sy bsd.device.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/devicecmp.fidl ;
see
.Xr fidl 7 .
The first call on a session checks the provider's wire version and fails
with
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT
for an incompatible provider.
A call is bounded by 30 seconds, shortened by an active
.Fn service_budget_begin
budget
.Pq Xr service_runtime 3 ;
a timeout, transport failure or malformed reply
.Pq Er EPROTO
drops the cached session, and the next call opens a new one.
Application errors such as
.Er EACCES
keep it.
.In devicecmp_protocol.h
still defines the rights bits, list entry and limits; its message records
are the pre-FIDL wire format and are rejected by the current provider.
.Pp
.Fn devicecmp_list
enumerates the devices the caller's label is permitted to open, so a consumer
can discover its openable set without blind trial-and-error.
The result is data-only: no descriptor is delivered.
It is owner-scoped by the provider to the caller's own unforgeable channel
label, never a wire argument, so a caller can only observe entries whose policy
label equals its own; a label with no policy lists empty (returns 0 with
.Fa *countp
set to zero).
.Fa cursor
is zero for the first page or a prior call's
.Fa *next_cursor ;
up to
.Fa max
entries (capped by the provider at
.Dv DEVICECMP_LIST_MAX
per page) are written to
.Fa entries ,
with
.Fa *countp
set to the number filled and
.Fa *next_cursor
set to the cursor to re-issue for the next page
.Pq zero on the final page .
If
.Fa max
is smaller than the returned page, the call fails with
.Er ENOMEM ,
stores the required entry count in
.Fa *countp ,
and does not advance the cursor.
Each
.Vt struct devicecmp_list_entry
carries the device leaf
.Va name ,
the policy-maximum
.Va rights
mask for
.Pq this label, this device ,
and a
.Va flags
word whose
.Dv DEVICECMP_LIST_FLAG_IOCTL_WHITELIST
bit notes that a per-device ioctl whitelist further narrows a delivered
.Dv DEVICECMP_RIGHT_IOCTL
descriptor.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std devicecmp_open devicecmp_list
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr service_runtime 3 ,
.Xr fidl 7 ,
.Xr BSDDevice 8
